UNIT adopt_kept_content
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@c7b0916ed08a cli/lib/adopt.mts extractKeptContent (with its helpers linesOf, stripMarkers, extractItems,
  splitSentences, normalizeForMatch, norm, isFence, isOneLineFence, fenceRun, closesFence), reproduced exactly,
  accidents included. Every character fact below was probed on the pinned code.
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG adopt_kept_content(unmanaged: text, managed: text) -> list[text]
ENVELOPE text only, both arguments: any length (empty included), any characters (NUL included), LF / CRLF / CR / U+2028
  line content, every BLANK character. A non-text argument is outside the abstract envelope (the package throws; see the
  js file).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION During rdv init --adopt, the giver's own rule text (unmanaged) is about to be replaced by the profile's
  managed section (managed). The managed text is cut to its marker block and both texts are read into ITEMS (R1-R5): a
  heading, a list item or paragraph with its wrapped lines joined, or a fenced code block kept whole. The result is every
  unmanaged item that is not REPRESENTED by the managed text (R9), rendered whole (R10), in reading order. An item is
  represented only when ONE managed item says all of it: the sentences of a prose item appearing as one unbroken run, in
  the same order, among one managed prose item's sentences, or a fence's key lines (its opener included) equal, in order, to one managed
  fence's. Nothing is ever deduplicated; over-keeping is by design.
BEHAVIOR
R1 Preparation. LF-READ of a text: split it at every LF, remove ONE CR from the end of each piece that ends in CR, and
  rejoin with LF. The UNMANAGED text is LF-READ, then BLANK is trimmed from both ends of the whole text; markers are NOT
  looked for in it (a marker in the giver's text is ordinary text). The MANAGED text is LF-READ; then, if BEGIN occurs,
  everything up to and including its leftmost occurrence is removed; then, if END occurs in what remains, its leftmost
  occurrence and everything after it are removed; then BLANK is trimmed from both ends. BEGIN = "<!--", zero or more
  BLANK (LF included), "rederive:begin" not followed by a WORD character, then everything up to and including the
  FIRST "-->" after "rederive:begin" (possibly spanning lines), provided no other "<!--" begins between the end of
  "rederive:begin" and that "-->"; a candidate whose first following "-->" comes after another "<!--" (or that has no
  following "-->") is not a begin marker, and a later candidate may be. END = "<!--", zero or more BLANK, "rederive:end", zero or more
  BLANK, "-->" (it may span lines). Text joined across a removed marker is joined directly. A trim makes an indented
  first line no longer indented when R3 classifies it CITED FIDELITY package — stripMarkers, extractKeptContent
R2 Lines of a prepared text: split it at every LF and remove ONE CR from the end of each line that ends in CR (a second
  pass after R1: a line that originally ended in two CRs before its LF loses both, three loses two). CR elsewhere,
  U+2028 and U+2029 stay inside their line. "The trimmed line" = the line with BLANK removed from both ends. ONE-LINE
  FENCE (grammar R4) is tested first; a FENCE LINE is a line that is not a ONE-LINE FENCE and whose trimmed form starts
  with three backticks or with three tildes (four or more, an info string, or anything after them included). The RUN of
  a FENCE LINE is the maximal leading run of its trimmed form's first character (three or more backticks, or three or
  more tildes). A line CLOSES an open fence when its trimmed form consists only of the opening line's RUN character,
  repeated at least as many times as that RUN is long (no info string, nothing else; indentation and trailing BLANK are
  allowed) CITED FIDELITY package — linesOf, isFence, fenceRun, closesFence
R3 Items: walk the lines in order with at most one OPEN prose item (a marker, possibly empty, and a body) or one OPEN
  fence (a list of lines), never both. "Close" = if a prose item is open and its body contains a non-BLANK character it
  becomes the next item; either way none is open afterwards. Each line is the first class below that applies:
  IN-FENCE (a fence is open) — append the line as read to the fence; if the line CLOSES it (R2), the fence becomes the
  next item and is no longer open. Every other line is content, whatever it looks like: backticks inside a tilde fence,
  a shorter run, a run with an info string, a heading, a marker. A line of seven or more backticks only (a ONE-LINE
  FENCE when outside a fence) closes a backtick fence whose RUN it is not shorter than. ONE-LINE FENCE — close; the line is itself the next fence item
  (lines: just this line). FENCE LINE — close; open a fence whose first line is this line. HEADING (grammar R4) —
  close; the next item is a heading item. BLANK LINE (the trimmed line is empty) — close. LIST (grammar R5) — close,
  then open a prose item with the matched MARKER and BODY. TEXT — if a prose item is open, append one space and the
  trimmed line to its body; otherwise open a prose item with an empty marker and the trimmed line as body. At the end
  of the text: close; then an open fence (never closed) becomes the last item with the lines it has
  CITED FIDELITY package — extractItems
R4 ONE-LINE FENCE: the trimmed line starts with three backticks, ends with three backticks, has at least one character
  between them (seven backticks qualify, six do not) and contains no NOTDOT character; tildes never make one (a
  "~~~x~~~" line is a FENCE LINE). HEADING (the whole line): one to six "#" at the start, then a non-empty run of
  BLANK, then a non-empty tail containing no NOTDOT character; the run and the tail may divide anywhere that works (so
  NOTDOT may occur only inside the BLANK run after the hashes). Seven "#", "#x" and an indented "#" line (other than
  the first line, see R1) are not headings; "#" followed by two spaces IS (an empty heading). A heading item's marker
  is its hashes and its body is everything after the hashes with BLANK trimmed CITED FIDELITY package
R5 LIST (the whole line): zero or more BLANK, then MARKER = one or more ASCII digits followed by ".", or "-", or "*";
  then one or more BLANK; then BODY = everything after that maximal BLANK run, which must contain no NOTDOT character.
  BODY is kept untrimmed (its trailing BLANK stays). So "-" alone, "1)", "+", "--", "*emph*" and a list line with a
  NOTDOT after its body start are TEXT; "- " opens an item with an empty body that later TEXT lines continue (its body
  then starts with the joining space); "007." is a MARKER kept as written CITED FIDELITY package
R6 Fragments (sentences) of a prose or heading body. If the body contains a NUL or a SPANMARK character it is not cut:
  its one fragment is the body with BLANK trimmed from both ends. Otherwise, in three steps. MASK: scanning left to right, each CODE SPAN (a
  backtick, zero or more non-backtick characters, a backtick; leftmost first, non-overlapping) is replaced by NUL, its
  0-based index in decimal ASCII digits, NUL; the spans are remembered in order. CUT the masked body at every maximal
  run of BLANK characters that is immediately preceded by a TERMINAL character, or by a CLOSER character itself
  immediately preceded by a TERMINAL character, AND is immediately followed by a START character; the run is removed.
  So a cut never falls inside a code span, a run before a code span counts as followed by a START, and a run after one
  never counts as preceded by a TERMINAL. UNMASK each piece: each mask token in it is replaced by the span it stands
  for (a cut never divides a token, and the body had no NUL of its own, so every token is the mask's; no fragment of this
  branch contains NUL or SPANMARK). Then each piece
  has BLANK trimmed from both ends and empty pieces are dropped. Nothing else cuts ("e.g. Foo" cuts; ". lower", ". É",
  ".)) X" and a run followed by a quote do not) CITED FIDELITY package — splitSentences
R7 KEY of a fragment. A fragment containing a NUL or a SPANMARK character: its KEY is RAWMARK followed by the fragment
  exactly as it is (no case mapping, no whitespace change). Any other fragment: pair the backticks from the left (first
  with second, third with fourth, ...); each pair with the text between them is a CODE SPAN, everything else (a last
  unpaired backtick included) is PROSE. Rewrite each code span as SPANMARK, the text between its backticks unchanged,
  SPANMARK (so the span's boundary survives: "`ls` -s" and "`ls -s`" differ). Rewrite each prose piece, on its own, by
  the Unicode default full lowercase mapping; every other character stays, asterisks included ("**bold**" and "bold"
  differ). Concatenate in order; replace every maximal BLANK run by one space; trim spaces from both ends. LINE KEY of
  a line: replace every maximal BLANK run by one space and trim; nothing else changes (case and asterisks stay) CITED
  FIDELITY package — normalizeForMatch, norm
R8 Managed shapes, from the items of the prepared managed text. Each non-fence managed item (headings included) gives
  one PROSE LIST: the KEYs of its fragments, in order. Each managed fence item gives one BLOCK KEY. The KEY LINES of
  a fence item: a ONE-LINE FENCE item -> its one line; a CLOSED fence (one whose last line CLOSES it, R2) -> its opener
  line (info string included), then every line strictly between opener and closer that is not blank (trimmed empty),
  in order; an UNCLOSED fence (it reached the end of the text, a lone opener included) -> no key lines. Lines between
  are key lines whatever they look like (a nested fence, an info-string line, a run of backticks); the closer never is.
  Its BLOCK KEY is the LINE KEYs of its key lines, in order, joined with LF (so "```" and "~~~" openers differ, and
  "```bash" differs from "```py").
  Keys compare as exact text equality; there is no other tolerance.
R9 An unmanaged item is REPRESENTED when: a prose or heading item — it has at least one fragment and the KEYs of its
  fragments, in order, occur as a CONTIGUOUS run of at least one PROSE LIST (no managed fragment between them; a
  repeated fragment needs as many adjacent equal managed fragments; an item with no fragments, i.e. an empty heading, is
  never represented); a fence item — it has at least one
  key line AND its BLOCK KEY equals the BLOCK KEY of some managed fence item (an unclosed fence is never represented; a
  closed fence with nothing between its delimiters has its opener as its one key line and can be). Prose is never represented by a fence nor a fence by prose.
R10 Result: for each unmanaged item in order that is not represented, one element: a fence item -> its lines joined
  with LF (a one-line fence -> its trimmed line); a prose or heading item with a non-empty marker -> the marker, one
  space, the body; otherwise the body. Equal items are each kept. The result may be empty.
PINNED charset BLANK = the code points 0009 000A 000B 000C 000D 0020 00A0 1680 2000 to 200A 2028 2029 202F 205F 3000 FEFF (hexadecimal, 25 members) CITED ECMA-262 WhiteSpace and LineTerminator (the package's whitespace class and trim); NOT Unicode White_Space (FEFF in, 0085 out)
PINNED charset NOTDOT = the code points 000D 2028 2029 CITED ECMA-262 LineTerminator minus LF (LF never occurs inside a line): the characters the package's heading, list and one-line-fence grammars cannot match
PINNED charset WORD = ASCII "A" to "Z", "a" to "z", "0" to "9" and "_" CITED the word boundary after "rederive:begin"
PINNED charset TERMINAL = "." "!" "?" CITED splitSentences
PINNED charset CLOSER = the double quote, the apostrophe ', ")" and "]" (0022 0027 0029 005D) CITED splitSentences
PINNED charset START = ASCII "A" to "Z", the backtick (0060), "(" and NUL (0000) CITED splitSentences — no other capital letter; NUL is the mask's lead
PINNED charset SPANMARK = the code point 0001 CITED normalizeForMatch — the boundary written around a code span's text
PINNED charset RAWMARK = the code point 0002 CITED normalizeForMatch — the prefix of a raw KEY
INVARIANT every element of the result is an unmanaged item rendered by R10; the result's order is the unmanaged reading order.
INVARIANT the managed text only ever removes elements: with an empty managed text every unmanaged item is kept.
ORACLE value -> oracles/adopt_kept_content.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 982e9570796b15f063574fcfe93ee3947ac7b2425e8fd24344f252302cf0475c
SCOPE IN: R1-R10 over any two texts. OUT: findUnmanagedDuplicate and replaceUnmanagedBlock (separate units), heading
  normalization (not used here), non-text arguments (js file only).
PROVENANCE rederive@c7b0916ed08a (MIT), cli/lib/adopt.mts lines 22-47 and 164-252: extractKeptContent and its private
  helpers. Re-cut 2026-10-27 for REDERIVE_SELF_PLAN round 5 (FINDINGS #91), delta re-cut round 5b (FINDINGS #118 #110),
  delta re-cut round 5c (FINDINGS #135 #123), delta re-cut round 5d (FINDINGS #144),
  delta re-cut round 5e (FINDINGS #146).
